A resource key with no value is not a failure, and three keys that stat already answers.

CFURLCopyResourcePropertyForKey returned FALSE whenever it produced no value, leaving the error it
was handed still NULL. macOS answers true with the value NULL when a property is simply not defined
for the URL and keeps false for a real error, which it then reports.

THIRTEEN OF THE THIRTY EIGHT KEYS in CFURLCreatePropertyForKey have no implementation at all, so
every one of them told the caller its request had FAILED, silently:

  IsVolume IsPackage VolumeURL LocalizedTypeDescription LabelNumber LabelColor LocalizedLabel
  EffectiveIcon CustomIcon FileResourceIdentifier VolumeIdentifier FileSecurity FileResourceType

This is not theoretical. getResourceValue:forKey:error: is referenced from six images inside
iA Writer, four inside Swift Publisher, three inside iTerm2 and one in MoneyMoney. Four of iA
Writer six are Sparkle; the other two are its own Kit and FoundationAdditions frameworks, which is
where the nil bookmark came from. It is the same shape as the bookmark family in
0131: nil with no error is a combination macOS never produces, and a caller is entitled to read it
as no failure.

The fix keeps its own error, so a caller that passes NULL for the error still gets the right
Boolean.

Three of the thirteen are answered here because one stat already has everything they need.
FileResourceIdentifier is documented as opaque and compared with CFEqual, so device and inode
together are exactly its promise: equal for two paths that link to the same file and unequal
otherwise. VolumeIdentifier is the device. FileResourceType is the S_IFMT bits mapped to the
constants CFURLConstants.c already defines, and it reads the link ITSELF with lstat, because stat
would report what a symlink points at and NSURLFileResourceTypeSymbolicLink could never be
answered.

The remaining ten stay unimplemented, and they now say so honestly rather than reporting failure.

